ortgage Applications Fail in New York?

The Bronx, more than anywhere else in the state of New York.

| County | Denial Rate | Applications |
|---|---|---|
| Bronx | 21.3% | 2,271 |
| Queens | 15.4% | 9,112|
| Brooklyn | 14.4% | 6,938 |
| Manhattan | 12.5% | 5,211 |
| Rockland | 10.4% | 2,254 |
| Staten Island | 9.9% | 3,108 |
| Westchester | 9.3% | 7,034 |
| Nassau | 9.0% | 9,250 |
| Onondaga | 8.7% | 4,002 |
| Suffolk | 8.4% | 11,865 |
| Erie | 8.1% | 7,968 |
| Albany | 7.7% | 2,509 |
| Monroe | 5.8% | 6,571 |

21.3% is higher than every rural upstate county. It is two and a half times higher than Suffolk while Suffolk has the highest applications of any county in the data.

### Is it just is poorer?

That was the first thing I checked and it is not.

![Denial rate by income band, Bronx above Suffolk in every band](images/income_bands.png)

|  Household Income | Bronx | Suffolk | Ratio |
|---|---|---|---|
| under $50k | 56.3% | 49.0% | 1.1x |
| $50-$100k | 30.1% | 17.9% | 1.7x |
| $100-$150k | 21.1% | 8.1% | 2.6x |
| $150-$250k | 14.3% | 6.0% | 2.4x |
| $250k+ | 12.4% | 5.8% | 2.1x |

The gap is there in every band and it is widest in the middle. A Bronx household earning $100-$150k gets denied more often than a Suffolk household earning half that. Even households earning over $250k are still denied at more than twice of Suffolk's rate.




